ame>Koh</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Eng Chia</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Koh</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A cross-sectional study of primary care physicians in Singapore on their concerns and preparedness for an avian influenza outbreak</article-title><source>Annals Academy of Medicine Singapore</source><year>2008</year><volume>37</volume><fpage>458</fpage><lpage>464</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kreuger</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name><name><surname>Casey</surname><given-names>MA</given-names></name></person-group><source>Focus Groups</source><year>2000</year><publisher-name>London; Sage</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B22"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kitzinger</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Barbour</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Barbour R, Kitzinger R</surname></name></person-group><article-title>Introduction: The Challenge and Promise of Focus Groups</article-title><source>Developing Focus Group Research: Politics, Theory and Practice</source><year>1999</year><publisher-name>London; Sage</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B23"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Morgan</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Kreuger</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Morgan D</surname></name></person-group><article-title>When to use focus groups and why</article-title><source>Successful Focus Groups: Advancing the State of the Art</source><year>1993</year><publisher-name>London; Sage</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B24"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bloor</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Frankland</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Thomas</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Robson</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name></person-group><source>Focus Groups in Social Research</source><year>2001</year><publisher-name>London; Sage</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B25"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Silverman</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><source>Interpreting qualitative data: Methods for analysing talk, text and interaction</source><year>2004</year><publisher-name>Sage; London</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B26"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Strauss</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Corbin</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><source>Basics of Qualitative Research, Grounded Theory Procedures</source><year>1990</year><publisher-name>London; Sage</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B27"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Finch</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Lewis</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Ritchie J, Lewis J</surname></name></person-group><article-title>Focus Groups</article-title><source>Qualitative Research Practice: A guide for Social Science Students and Researchers</source><year>2005</year><publisher-name>Sage; London</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B28"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bowling</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><source>Research Methods in Health: Investigating health and health