e="author"><name><surname>Wong</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Kolditz</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Millen</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Potter</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><source>Why they fight: combat motivation in the Iraq war</source><year>2003</year><publisher-name>DIANE publishing</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B33"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>MacCoun</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Kier</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Belkin</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Does social cohesion determine motivation in combat? An old question with an old answer</article-title><source>Armed Forces and Society</source><year>2005</year><volume>32</volume><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>9</lpage></citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>